Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.750 align:start position:0%
 
have you ever noticed that the police  cars in your country are almost always  made by national automakers in america  they use ford chevrolet and dodge in  germany they use bmw audi and volkswagen  in japan they use nissan toyota and so  on and so forth this makes sense  countries want to support national  automakers because this creates jobs  financially backs automakers to rough  times and displaced national pride  throughout the police force yet despite  tesla being american and the most valued  car company in the world the american  police doesn't really bother with teslas  now of course there are examples of  certain counties around the us using a  few teslas here and there but for the  most part the american police has stuck  to their routes with over 50 percent of  police vehicles still being manufactured  by ford so here's why the american  police shy away from using teslas  starting off with the surface level  reasons we have customization police  cars aren't a new invention by any means  the first police car dates back to 1899  when a police department in ohio started  testing an electric-powered wagon  throughout the early 1900s many police  departments around the us started  playing around with their own police  vehicles but all of these were  individual ventures for example a police  commissioner in detroit named frank  crawl had the idea of creating police  cars he asked the city if they would  invest in police vehicles but they  declined the opportunity so frank ended  up investing 5 000 of his own money to  buy a packard and modded for police use  after seeing the result detroit actually  purchased six more vehicles from frank  and this led to many other sectors in  detroit adopting cars as well  these cars weren't for chasing criminals  though originally the purpose of these  police cars was to improve the  effectiveness of each police officer  with their car each police officer could  cover a lot more area which saved a lot  of money long term but getting your  hands on police vehicles wasn't an easy  process by any means for the most part  police departments and chiefs had to  take on the responsibility of modding  cars themselves and this was often an  extremely long and tedious process it  took a police officer named kenneth cox  six years to figure out how to install a  radio into the back seat of a ford model  t  now given that he's a police officer and  not a car mechanic i don't blame him but  i think it's easy to see why police  departments found this customization  process quite annoying this all changed  in 1950 when ford introduced something  called the police package ford had been  taking notes on the most common mods  police departments had made to their  vehicles and ford combined all of these  mods into the police package five years  later in 1955 chevrolet came out with  their own police package and dodge  followed behind in 1956  and with that police departments were  finally able to get out of the modding  business after 50 years  most police departments had no urge to  return to the business but today if they  want to use a tesla that's exactly what  they have to do  the fremont police department is one of  the few counties that use a few teslas  in their fleet and they actually made a  list of all the mods they had to make to  the tesla this includes overhead light  bars and sirens police vehicle radios  prisoner partitions and so on and so  forth i'm sure tesla will eventually  come out with the police package but  that's simply not a top priority right  now  and that brings us to the second issue  which is scale despite being the most  valid car company in the world tesla is  still quite a small automaker when it  comes to vehicle output tesla is  increasing their production  exponentially year after year but  they're still yet to crack even one  million cars per year which means that  they still have a lot of room to grow  within the consumer market itself so it  makes little sense to tend to niche  markets like the police or fire  department when you still have so much  primary growth left i mean the legacy  american automakers ignored the police  department for 50 years it wasn't until  their first car boom settled down that  they turned their focus onto the police  department  so we may not see a tesla police package  until the first ev adoption boom is  behind us moving on likely the most  obvious factor holding back tesla  adoption is price though tesla likes to  market prices like 35 000 for the model  3 this is simply not true  to get that price they factor in a  lifetime of gas savings and any tax  incentives they can find  that price also does not include the  1200 delivery fee associated with buying  a tesla not to mention you have to pay  extra to have any color other than white  assuming that most police departments  want a black car that tacks on another  one thousand dollars onto the purchase  price  and what about range for highway patrol  cars the range is a pretty important  consideration the starting price of the  long range model three is fifty thousand  dollars add in everything we just talked  about and you're looking at about fifty  five thousand dollars and don't forget  we haven't even factored in the police  specific mods that generally cost about  ten thousand dollars so if you want a  decent model 3 patrol car you're looking  at a total out-of-pocket cost of 65  grand to put that in perspective the  2021 ford explorer police interceptor  utility starts at just 42 grand  even if you pump it up with some options  you're only looking at a total cost of  50 grand  not only is the ford explorer 15 000  cheaper but it's an suv as opposed to a  sedan  over the past decade the american police  have really been embracing suvs due to  their superior interior space higher  seating position and a greater carrying  capacity tesla doesn't make a proper suv  and so the only benefit you get from the  tesla is that it's electric if you want  self-driving the price disparity would  grow to 25 000  i know a lot of you guys will mention  that the tesla will be cheaper to run  and maintain and that they'll cost about  the same over the long term  there's no arguing against the low  maintenance bill of the tesla but the  gas savings may not actually be that  significant for police departments  here's the thing police cars don't pay  the same amount for gas as we do in most  cases the police department has its own  gas pumps and they're able to source  discounted subsidized gas through the  government smaller departments without  gas pumps often have deals with local  gas stations to get cheaper gas  something else to consider is that  police departments are exempt from  certain taxes which also reduces the gas  bill but even if we ignored all of this  and gave tesla the benefit of the doubt  and said that the tesla would be the  same price over the long term the  decision likely wouldn't change with the  tight police budgets and sometimes even  budget deficits the upfront cost of  purchasing the vehicles is simply a  bigger concern than the long-term cost  those are the logical reasons that the  police have thus far stayed away from  tesla but there's a couple of political  and bureaucratic factors as well  starting with social backlash in america  there is a demographic of people who  don't particularly like the police  in fact many of them think that police  funding should be cut and that the  department should be abolished  altogether  now of course that's the extreme end of  the spectrum but even people who are  relatively in the middle often don't  want to splurge on the police department  and while you may be able to objectively  support investing into eb police  vehicles for long-term cost benefits as  well as environmental benefits the same  cannot be said about the general public  tesla has an image of being the cool car  company they have self-driving cars they  have modern interiors they support  leading-edge technology and the company  is led by elon musk this positive public  image has done the company quite a bit  of good as consumers view tesla as a  luxury car maker and desire their cars  though the general public desires teslas  they don't see it as a necessity they  see it as a nice to have luxury product  and many of them don't like seeing tax  dollars being spent on nice to have  luxuries the counties that have bought  teslas for police use have experienced  the backlash first hand take the  hastings on hudson police department in  new york for example in december they  took delivery of a model white police  car and posted about it on twitter they  explained that the purchase would help  the department move towards  environmental sustainability and that  they would actually save money on gas  long term but twitter users weren't  quite convinced this user replied this  is what we mean when we say defund the  police all that extra money can be used  to better the communities they serve  this other user replied this wtf we mean  when we say defund the police people  starving and struggling to pay the rent  and somehow you fit tesla's in the  budget there were dozens of replies just  like these and whether this criticism is  deserved or not is besides the point the  truth is the criticism has more to do  with the brand of their car than the  price of the car the hudson police  department could have bought a more  expensive ford or chevrolet and no one  would have cared because those brands  don't carry the same social status as  tesla's so the easy way to avoid this  drama is simply avoiding teslas and that  brings me into the final and possibly  the most influential factor which is the  bureaucratic nature of government the  government is extremely slow when it  comes to change regardless of how big or  small the change is and how big or small  the benefits are the government tends to  embrace a policy of if it ain't broke  don't fix it for example in the 1980s  the police settled in on a favorite  vehicle the ford crown victoria i bet  all of you guys are familiar with this  vehicle as it was extremely popular for  such a long period of time  law enforcement in the us and canada  used the crown victoria as their  dominant police vehicle for 19 years  that's literally as long as the entire  company of tesla has been around i'm  sure that many police departments have  existing partnerships connections and  deals with legacy automakers which just  further dissuades them from switching to  tesla something else to consider is that  ford has been coming out with some  outstanding evs the maki has been a  massive success and it looks like the  ford f-150 lightning is actually going  to hit the market before the cyber truck  also the maquis just became the first  all-electric vehicle to pass the  michigan state police pursuit vehicle  test last week and ford is already  looking to build purpose-built electric  police vehicles so ford is by no means  ready to give up the police market  combine this with all the factories we  already outlined and i don't think we'll  be seeing the police using tesla and  mass anytime soon do you disagree  comment that down below  also drop a like if you're glad that  tesla is at the very least pushing  forward ev adoption within the police  force and of course consider joining our  discord community to suggest future  video ideas and consider subscribing to  see more questions logically answered  but until then i'm ari and i'll see you  guys on the next one

